# Inset Text

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

Inset text sets one piece of supporting information apart from the text around it, such as what an estimate means or what a setting does and does not change. Use it sparingly, on help pages and reference dashboards.

For urgent conditions use [Status and notifications](status.html). For tips and alerts see [Alert & Tip Callout](alert-and-tip-callout.html). For background that can be skipped, see [Aside](aside.html).

This page covers:

- Inset Text
  - Civica Inset Text Pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

An inset has two parts:

- A bar down the left edge of the card, 10 pixels wide.
- The text, with space around it, in the normal reading order.

The bar is decoration. It is not a button and does nothing when pressed.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-inset-text-pattern"><code>Civica Inset Text Pattern</code></h3>

Home Assistant has no inset card. Civica builds the pattern from a Markdown card (`type: markdown`) and a card-mod style, so it **needs the card-mod integration**. Without card-mod the card is an ordinary Markdown card.

```yaml
type: markdown
content: >
  Note: It can take up to 10 minutes for the thermostat to report its new state.
card_mod:
  style: |
    ha-card {
      background: transparent;
      border: none;
      border-left: 10px solid var(--divider-color);
      border-radius: 0;
      padding: 15px;
      box-shadow: none;
    }
```

The ready-made file is `cards/inset-text.yaml`. Edit the text and keep the style.

**What the Civica theme sets.** The bar takes `--divider-color`, which is #CECECE in light mode and #50565A in dark mode. The text takes `primary-text-color`.

**What the bar can and cannot do.** Calculated from the theme colours, the bar is about 1.6 to 1 against a white card, and about 2 to 1 against the dark card surface. That is below the 3 to 1 that a line needs to stand out on its own. So treat the bar as decoration. Write the text so that it makes sense without it.

**Start with a visible word.** Begin with a word such as “Note:” so that the sentence announces itself. A Markdown card cannot add a visually hidden word without raw HTML, so do not plan on one.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- It uses a native Markdown card, so the text stays editable in the dashboard editor.
- It needs card-mod. If card-mod is not installed, the bar and the transparent background are missing.
- The bar is faint, so it cannot carry meaning on its own.
- It is static text, not a live message. It is never the place for a change that people must notice.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use one idea in each inset, in plain words, next to the thing it explains. Never use an inset as the only warning of an urgent condition. Do not stack insets. When several compete with the headings, turn some into ordinary text. Test the dashboard with the bar hidden, and check the text in light and dark modes.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the visible word says what kind of information it is, not only the bar.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: the bar is never the only signal.
- 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum): check the text on your own dashboard.
- 1.4.4 Resize Text and 1.4.10 Reflow: the text must wrap.
- 2.4.6 Headings and Labels: put the inset under the heading it explains.
- 3.1.5 Reading Level: use plain words.

One idea, in plain words, keeps the effort low.

